    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-aranya-kanda-0043",
      "sequence": 749,
      "meaning": "Knowing Rāma to be greatly pleased, Nārada again spoke gently: ‘Rāma, when you impelled your own māyā and deluded me, hear me, king of Raghu, I wished to marry. Lord, why did you not allow it?’ Rāma replied affectionately, ‘Hear, sage: I always protect those who worship me after relinquishing every other reliance, just as a mother protects her child. When an infant runs to grasp fire or a snake, its mother restrains and saves it. Once that child matures, the mother loves him but does not act as she did before. The wise are like my adult sons; a humble servant is like my infant child. My devotee relies on my strength, while the wise person relies on his own; desire and anger are enemies of both. Understanding this, learned people worship me and, even after attaining knowledge, do not abandon devotion. Desire, anger, greed, pride and powerful delusion constitute māyā's army; among them, the passage presents woman in the form of māyā as especially fierce and sorrow-giving.’",
