    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-aranya-kanda-0044",
      "sequence": 750,
      "meaning": "The text continues through a series of inherited, explicitly gendered metaphors, saying that Purāṇas, Vedas and saints call woman springtime for the forest of delusion; summer that dries every reservoir of recitation, austerity and discipline; the one rainy season that delights the frogs of desire, anger, pride and envy; autumn that always pleases clusters of night-lotuses of corrupt inclination; winter frost that scorches every lotus of dharma and weakens happiness; the cold season in which thickets of the javāsa plant of possessiveness flourish; a dense dark night pleasing to flocks of the owls of sin; and a fish-hook for the fish of intellect, strength, character and truth. Calling the alluring woman the root of fault, giver of pain and mine of suffering, Rāma says he prevented Nārada's marriage because he understood this in his heart.",
